# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ PUZZLE 1 · Up an octave, down in volume              │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# This tone is a bit loud and a bit low. Change it so it is
# exactly one octave higher, and at half the volume.
#
# Check your answer:   edc/learn check 1
# Hear your attempt:   edc/learn puzzle 1
#
#@ top 440 3 -- one octave above 220 Hz
#@ rms< -20 -- half the volume (level below -20 dB)
#@ rms> -26 -- ...but not much quieter than half

play tone:
  sin(TAU * phasor(220)) * 0.2

tone

#
#
#
#
#
#
#
# ▶ HINTS (scroll down only if you're stuck)
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#   An octave up means doubling the frequency: the clock
#   laps twice as often.
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#
#   Half the volume means halving the number you multiply
#   by. (Every halving of a signal is about -6 dB.)
